  "body": "## Conditional improvement\n\n$$T(n)=O\\big(n(\\log n)^{1-\\kappa}\\big),\\qquad \\kappa=\\frac{6558894}{10^{10}}=6.558894\\times10^{-4}.$$\n\nThis is **+42.30% over #144** (4.609169e-4, merged), +7.00% over #173 (6.1297e-4, the best other open PR) and +10.42% over #163. It supersedes this PR's earlier versions (6.096379e-4, 6.457084e-4 and 6.489120e-4). The complex side binds: its saving is 6563200/10\u00b9\u2070, and the bit coarse saving is 6600253/10\u00b9\u2070 (6.5961531e-4 effective). All of #144's interfaces remain assumptions.\n\n## Change\n\nThis branch contains #161's construction (#161 is closed in favor of this PR) and adds the following.\n\n| side | word | R | W per vertex | saving |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| complex | #161 | 16,011 | 15,681 | 5885669/10\u00b9\u2070 |\n| complex | **this** | **13,372** | **13,655** | **6563200/10\u00b9\u2070** (+11.51%) |\n| bit | #161 | 23,368 | 26,888 | 5936323/10\u00b9\u2070 (coarse) |\n| bit | **this** | **19,788 (18,028 physical)** | **21,548** | **6600253/10\u00b9\u2070** (coarse, +11.18%) |\n\n### Complex word (p = 11)\n\n- **Modules.** The triple-disjoint, pair-disjoint and all-but-one modules are pinned annealed modules in `references/paired-cube/sources`. Each is loaded with its contract checked (`triple_module_from`, `pair_module_from`, `all_but_one_from`), in place of PR117's restrictions and #144's balanced tree. The all-but-one module was annealed from a nested prefix:\n  - prefix sums p_{k+1} = p_k + x_k and suffix sums s_k = x_k + s_{k+1};\n  - outputs y_0 = s_1, y_{n\u22121} = p_{n\u22121} and y_{i+1} = p_i + (x_i + s_{i+2});\n  - each prefix is used twice with nested supports, which admits more carrier links than the balanced tree.\n- **Local channel circuit (`local_L1.json`).** Every cube's 13 local outputs keep their values.\n  - G01 (both modes) and G12 (mode 1) become sums of two shared long-diagonal differences, and F is built on axis 2: 27 additions per cube instead of 25. The (0,1) edges lose their G duty, so the carrier matching gains links.\n  - `Graph(p, local=None)` runs #144's circuit unchanged.\n- **Fused outputs.** Each port's single-target face-2, edge-02 and edge-12 outputs are fused into one signed node read at 1/2, in a fixed fold order per port class (`merge_outputs`, variant `f8:00111100`). This follows #163's same-singleton fusion.\n- **Carrier arcs.** #162's `compile_closure` compiles the frozen 10,704 arcs in the main producer. #144's `verify.py` independently checks acyclicity and span containment. There are 2,310 rank-18 gauges.\n- **Physical layer.** 15,149 operation frames move in connected bundles that share a frame. The 2,310 gauged roles use late-read reuse (#124, #143), paired by maximum weight.\n- **Terminal sinks (`research/terminal-sinks`).** 47 all-eight side roots are terminal sinks under #166's lemma, and `sinks_gate.py` checks them.\n  - A sink's register starts at frame 0, is only ever a destination outside phase one, and has a pivot target p with no deferred read of y_p during its writes.\n  - The register is deleted. At the phase cut y_t \u2212= y_p for the other targets, every write goes to y_p at its own frame, and after the last write y_t += y_p at the root frame.\n  - The gate rechecks every sink condition, scans the literal stage words forward and reflected, runs a dirty-scratch replay mod 2\u2076\u00b9 \u2212 1, and rejects four controls.\n  - Per vertex: W = 13,655, deficit 1,320, largest child 20.\n\n### Bit word (`research/paired-cube-bit`, p = 12)\n\n- **Modules and merge.** The word uses an all-but-one module (n = 10, 33 additions, pinned in `data/qmod_p12.json`) annealed from the nested prefix, and an annealed pair module with 442 additions. Its local channels use the L1 association: the G sums with keys (0,1,0), (0,1,1) and (1,2,1) add the two long face diagonals. Variant u merges each cube's face-1/edge-01 outputs into u(b0,b1) and its face-2/edge-02 outputs into w(b0,b2); each merged node is read by two single roots. There are 3,960 gauges.\n- **Physical layer (new).** `scripts/paired_cube_bit_physical.py` applies the frozen word in `references/paired-cube/bit-physical`: late copies, 2,884 moved operation frames and 1,760 compensated late reads. It checks:\n  - register contents against the package graph;\n  - exact, G-nondegenerate frames;\n  - spliced role chains and pair chronology;\n  - target chains in read order and the partner chronology;\n  - the ledger, by recount;\n  - a 64-lane aliased F2 replay with three controls rejected.\n\n### Assembly\n\n- **Balanced prefix (`scripts/paired_cube_assembly.py`).** It uses the RaD project's reviewed balanced positional transform, with prefix margin 1 \u2212 \u03b5. The original prefix rejects this \u03ba.\n- **Atom exponent at the toll edge.** \u03b8 is the first 10\u207b\u00b9\u00b2 grid point above a_0/(1 + a_0 \u2212 a_old), as in #158: \u03b8 = 323062859/(5\u00b710\u00b9\u00b9).\n\n## Verification\n\n```sh\nmake paired-cube-verify\npython3 research/paired-cube-bit/paired_cube_bit_word.py --p 12 --check\npython3 research/paired-cube-bit/check_paired_cube_bit.py --dir research/paired-cube-bit/out --p 12\n```\n\n`make paired-cube-verify` runs the producer, the complex physical layer, the terminal-sink gate, the bit package checker, the bit physical layer and the network certificate.\n\n- **Complex.** Every coefficient of H + K + B = I, frames, intersections, target chains, and the complex physical layer's spliced chains, read-order target chains, telescoping deficit and aliased mod 2\u2076\u00b9 \u2212 1 replay with three controls.\n- **Bit.** The package checker (five mutation controls), byte-identical regeneration from frozen arcs, and the bit physical layer above.\n- **Assembly.** Both moments are strict, with the next grid points rejected. All 47 constraints and 7 margins hold.\n- Every command above passes on Linux with a clean working tree, as do the maintainer's paired-cube entry-point tests. The full suite runs in CI.\n\n## Credits\n\n- **Paired-cube decoder, modules, shared cores, bit ledger and assembly:** @icekylinx (#144, building on #130).\n- **Closure-compiled carrier arcs and pairing rounds:** @DaysSky (#162).\n- **Terminal-sink lemma:** @jamesyc (#166, closed by its author), audited here by its own gate.\n- **Same-singleton output fusion:** @chafreaky (#163); the fold orders here come from our own search.\n- **Atom exponent and phase stop at their edges:** @geckods (#158, #153).\n- **Balanced positional transform:** the RaD project (@hipotures, #20); balanced assembly composition @jamesyc (#34), with the reviewed parameters of Chafik Boukhalfa's `research/copied-fixed/balanced_assembly.py`.\n- **Physical frames and reuse on covers, late compensation reads:** eumemic, with OpenAI Codex assistance (#131, #143). **Birth-read reuse:** @jamesyc (#124). **Completed-core sharing:** @an664 (#128).\n- **Bit compile conventions:** Zhihao Chen (#97). **Bit lineage of the pair modules (#62):** Swapnil Jain.\n- **Annealed triple, pair and all-but-one modules, the nested prefix, the local-circuit search, output merging (w02, u, the f8 fold orders), bundle descent, both physical layers, the terminal-sink gate, the paired-cube bit word and the assembly integration:** eumemic, with Anthropic Claude assistance.\n- Connected frame moves also appear, as parallel work, in #164's bit-frame descents.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
